month; In particular we give a new proof of a theorem of Chudnovsky and Seymour, which states that the independence polynomial of a claw-free graph has only real zeros.\n### Abstract\n\nIn this talk I will introduce certain identities for independence polynomials, that are similar to the Christoffel--Darboux identities used in the theory of orthogonal polynomials. As an application, we will see that some conditions on the induced subgraphs of a graph G translates to results on zero-free regions of the independence polynomial of G. In particular we give a new proof of a theorem of Chudnovsky and Seymour, which states that the independence polynomial of a claw-free graph has only real zeros.\n\n### Video Recording\n\nChristoffel-Darboux Type Identities for the Independence Polynomial--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndependence Polynomial (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/IndependencePolynomial.html)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Li et al. (2026b) announced a proof for every finite forest. ... The independence polynomial of a claw-free graph is logarithmically concave (Chudnovsky and Seymour 2004).\nThe assertion that the independence polynomial of every tree is unimodal was a longstanding conjecture (Levit and Mandrescu 2005, Li et al. 2026a). Li et al. (2026b) announced a proof for every finite forest. They report that AI systems generated the new mathematical arguments and formal proofs in their paper. The independence polynomial of a claw-free graph is logarithmically concave (Chudnovsky and Seymour 2004).\n\nThe dependence polynomial counts the complementary vertex subsets and satisfies Image: D(G,x)=(1+x)^n-I(G,x), where Image: n is the vertex count (Xie and Xu 2026).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZero-free regions for the independence polynomial on restricted graph classes (https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1112/jlms.70